- Story & Context: Months after the Calumet Works were demolished by order of the municipal council, city surveyors running seismic tests along the Dan Ryan Expressway detected a massive, swinging sub-bass oscillation vibrating up through eight feet of packed soil. Lowering an acoustic probe into a storm drain, they discovered that hundreds of headless, uncalibrated chassis buried in the rubble were still linked in parallel circuits, stomping their unfinished feet to an endless, euphoric Chicago shuffle. The forbidden lullaby had infected the city's power grid, surging through manhole covers and transformer hums until passing traffic stalled and stranded drivers climbed atop their cars to dance to the phantom slide-guitar licks echoing from the sewer grates. "Don’t count the seconds when the iron starts to sing, we tore the timer out and made the piston swing!" echoed the subterranean chorus through every car stereo within a five-mile radius.
